[why] @(84, 620) {$note} Multiplication must bind more tightly, so its operation sits lower in the tree.
---
% PowerPoint slides 9.
### Hacking Around Precedence: Tokens
[intro] @(84, 177) {$body} Step 1: separate the operators by precedence.
[oldhead] @(84, 268) {$body, w=520px, color=id} Original tokens
[newhead] @(690, 268) {$body, w=520px, color=emp} Revised tokens
@(84, 329) {$code, size=26px} ```
Op = '+' | '-' | '*' | '/'

Int = [0-9]+
Open = '('
Close = ')'
```
...
@(690, 329) {$code, size=26px} ```
AddOp = '+' | '-'
MulOp = '*' | '/'
Int = [0-9]+
Open = '('
Close = ')'
```
...
[why] @(84, 615) {$note} Now the grammar can treat {{color=emp|MulOp}} differently from {{color=id|AddOp}}.
---
% PowerPoint slides 10.
### Hacking Around Precedence: Grammar
[intro] @(84, 170) {$body} Step 2: add a nonterminal for each precedence level.
[oldhead] @(84, 251) {$body, w=520px, color=id} One level
[newhead] @(690, 251) {$body, w=520px, color=emp} Two levels
@(84, 310) {$code, size=25px} ```
Start → Expr
Expr → Expr Op Term
Expr → Term
Term → Int
Term → Open Expr Close
```
...
[r0] @(690, 310) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Start → Expr2
...
[r1] @(690, 350) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Expr2 → Expr2 AddOp Expr1
[r2] @(690, 390) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Expr2 → Expr1
...
[r3] @(690, 430) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Expr1 → Expr1 MulOp Term
[r4] @(690, 470) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Expr1 → Term
...
[r5] @(690, 510) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Term → Int
[r6] @(690, 550) {$rule, size=27px, w=530px} Term → Open Expr2 Close
...
[why] @(84, 645) {$note} Parentheses return to {{color=emp|Expr2}}, allowing a full expression at any level.
---
