% PowerPoint slides 26–27.
### Predictive Parsing Table
[grammar] @(84, 160) {$rule, w=1112px, size=29px} S → E S′&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;S′ → + S | ε&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;E → num | ( S )

...
[table] @(84, 240) {$table, colwidths=(12%,20%,16%,20%,16%,16%)} |  | num | + | ( | ) | EOF |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| S | S → E S′ |  | S → E S′ |  |  |
| S′ |  | S′ → + S |  | S′ → ε | S′ → ε |
| E | E → num |  | E → ( S ) |  |  |
...
[empty] @(84, 509) {$note} A blank cell means {{color=neon_pink|syntax error}}. EOF marks the end of input, often written `$`.
...
[functions] @(84, 596) {$note} One procedure per nonterminal:{{br}}`parse_S()`, `parse_S_prime()`, and `parse_E()`.
% Use parse_S_prime: an apostrophe is not valid in a C++ identifier.
---
% PowerPoint slides 28.
### Implementing LL(1): parse_S
[grammar] @(84, 160) {$rule, w=1112px, size=29px} S → E S′&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;S′ → + S | ε&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;E → num | ( S )
@(84, 210) {$code, w=710px, size=27px} ```cpp
void parse_S() {
  switch (lexer.Peek()) {
    case num:
    case '(':
      parse_E();
      parse_S_prime();
      return;
    default:
      ParseError();
  }
}
```
...
[look] @(850, 217) {$note, w=340px} `Peek()` inspects{{br}}the lookahead token{{br}}without consuming it.
...
[rule] @(850, 379) {$note, w=340px} For `num` or `(`:{{br}}{{color=emp|S → E S′}}
...
[order] @(850, 507) {$note, w=340px} Call the procedures{{br}}in the order of the{{br}}right-hand side.
---
% PowerPoint slides 29.
### Implementing LL(1): parse_S_prime
[grammar] @(84, 160) {$rule, w=1112px, size=29px} S → E S′&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;S′ → + S | ε&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;E → num | ( S )
@(84, 210) {$code, w=710px, size=26px} ```cpp
void parse_S_prime() {
  switch (lexer.Peek()) {
    case '+':
      lexer.Use();
      parse_S();
      return;
    case ')':
    case EOF:
      return;
    default:
      ParseError();
  }
}
```
...
[plus] @(850, 214) {$note, w=340px} `+` selects{{br}}{{color=emp|S′ → + S}}.{{br}}Consume `+`, then{{br}}parse the next S.
...
[empty] @(850, 424) {$note, w=340px} `)` or EOF selects{{br}}{{color=emp|S′ → ε}}.{{br}}Return without{{br}}consuming a token.
---
% PowerPoint slides 30.
### Implementing LL(1): parse_E
[grammar] @(84, 160) {$rule, w=1112px, size=29px} S → E S′&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;S′ → + S | ε&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;E → num | ( S )
@(84, 210) {$code, w=730px, size=24px} ```cpp
void parse_E() {
  switch (lexer.Peek()) {
    case num: lexer.Use(); return;
    case '(':
      lexer.Use();
      parse_S();
      if (lexer.Peek() != ')')
        ParseError();
      lexer.Use();
      return;
    default:
      ParseError();
  }
}
```
...
[num] @(850, 203) {$note, w=340px} A number selects{{br}}{{color=emp|E → num}}.
...
[paren] @(850, 318) {$note, w=340px} `(` selects{{br}}{{color=emp|E → ( S )}}.{{br}}Consume `(`, recurse,{{br}}then require `)`.
...
[fresh] @(850, 502) {$note, w=340px} Check {{color=emp|fresh lookahead}}{{br}}after `parse_S()`{{br}}returns.
% Fix the stale-token check in the source: inspect Peek() after parsing S.
% As in the source, these are lexer-interface sketches. ParseError must not return.
